@@ -199,6 +199,8 @@ The text ("in every place incense is offered to my name, and a pure offering") i
 
 One honest caveat: some early fathers read Malachi 1:11 eucharistically, taking the offering to be the Eucharist. That reading is real, but it points toward the Eucharist as offering, not toward incense specifically, so it does not by itself deliver a command to burn incense; at most it relocates the discussion to the question of Eucharistic sacrifice (which touches Article 31), a separate topic.
 
+*(Dated addition, 260833-2, extending the caveat above rather than replacing it.)* A closer look at two of those early readings sharpens the caveat rather than settling it. Justin Martyr, writing around AD 155, quotes Malachi 1:10-12 in full, incense clause included, and identifies its fulfillment as the bread and the cup of the Eucharist; he draws no incense practice from a verse he has just quoted with the incense clause still in it. The Didache, commonly dated earlier or around the same time, quotes the "pure offering" language of the same verse but leaves the incense clause out entirely, and ties the purity it requires to reconciliation with one's neighbor rather than to anything ceremonial. Neither text gives incense a home in Christian worship; both give the Eucharist one. That should be said plainly, and so should the other half of it: both writers also call the Eucharistic assembly itself a sacrifice, applying this same verse to it, which supports reading Malachi 1:11 as a sacrifice text more than it settles anything about incense one way or the other. It would be an overclaim to cite these fathers only for the half that helps. One more caution, running the other direction: the Hebrew word behind "incense" in Malachi 1:11 is not the same word used for the compound incense of Exodus 30, of which frankincense is only one ingredient among four, so the two should not be treated as interchangeable. But both fathers were reading the verse in Greek, in a translation that already renders it "incense," so that distinction doesn't actually reach their reading, even though it matters for an argument built on the English text alone. And whether the church of this period burned incense in worship at all is a separate historical question this note doesn't settle either way; it deserves its own look before anyone leans on it.
