+*[Analysis throughout except where marked [Stated - primary source]. JD supplied the verbatim primary texts this session; nothing below has been independently re-verified against a critical edition by this pass — flag before quoting any patristic text outward. Cross-ref `LS-111` (the same verse, Eucharist-sacrifice register, Heschmeyer read-aloud + endorsing gloss), `DQ-19`(c)(ii) (the same verse, incense-warrant register, his own composition), §4.6 above. This section speaks to the Malachi 1:11 lever specifically and does not touch §4.1-4.5, §4.7-4.12.*
+
+**(a) Justin Martyr, *Dialogue with Trypho* 41 (c. AD 155). [Stated - primary source, as supplied]** Justin quotes Malachi 1:10-12 **including the incense clause**, and identifies the fulfilment as **the bread and the cup of the Eucharist** — not a ceremonial practice. The chapter runs the same type-antitype move three times in succession: the fine-flour offering of Lev 14:10 (the cleansed leper's offering) → the Eucharistic bread; the Malachi 1:11 "pure offering" → the bread and cup "which Jesus Christ has taught us to offer, in remembrance of the suffering"; circumcision on the eighth day → the circumcision of the heart. **He draws NO incense practice from a verse he has just quoted with the incense clause still in it.** This is the single sharpest datum against reading Malachi 1:11 as licensing a ceremonial incense practice, from a witness earlier than any attested Christian liturgical incense.
+
+**(b) Didache 14 (date disputed, commonly placed 1st-2nd c.). [Stated - primary source, as supplied]** The Didache also invokes Malachi (1:11/1:14, "in every place and time offer me a pure sacrifice") but **OMITS the incense clause entirely** — it quotes the "pure offering/sacrifice" language and stops short of the incense half of the verse. It identifies the "pure sacrifice" with the **Lord's Day assembly, the breaking of bread, and thanksgiving**, and attaches a **moral** purity condition (reconciliation with one's neighbour before offering) rather than a ceremonial one. ⚠️ **An omission proves less than an identification does, and this note weights it accordingly: the Didache is not independent evidence AGAINST an incense reading, only evidence that names nothing FOR one.**
+
+**(c) Justin Martyr, *Dialogue with Trypho* 117. [Stated - primary source, as supplied]** Justin returns to the same ground, refuting a rival reading (that the verse refers to the diaspora Jewish prayers of the dispersion), and grants that **prayers and thanksgivings are the only perfect and well-pleasing sacrifices**, claiming Christians alone rightly offer these — in the remembrance effected by the bread and the cup. ⚠️ **Read strictly, this restates and defends chapter 41 rather than adding an independent argument. Record it as ONE argument attested twice, plus one silent omission (the Didache) — NOT as three independent hits**, which is the overclaim this note exists to prevent.
+
+**(d) [Analysis] The remembrance-as-vehicle reading.** On this construction, the bread and cup are the **setting and occasion** within which prayer and thanksgiving are offered, not a second sacrificial substance offered alongside them. ⛔ **This is an INFERENCE from how Justin's sentences are put together, not a formal equation he himself states — label it [Analysis], never [Stated], and do not quote it as though it were his own formulation.**
+
+**(e) ⭐⭐⭐ THE HONEST COUNTERWEIGHT, RECORDED WITH EQUAL PROMINENCE TO (a)-(d), BECAUSE OMITTING IT WOULD BE EXACTLY THE OVERCLAIM THIS PROJECT'S DISCIPLINE EXISTS TO PREVENT.** These same texts **SUPPORT the Eucharist-is-sacrificial reading at `LS-111`.** Both Justin and the Didache call the Eucharistic assembly **a sacrifice and an offering**, in so many words, applying Malachi 1:11/1:14 to it directly. **The material is a MIXED WITNESS: it undercuts the incense-practice derivation from Malachi 1:11 while SUPPORTING the sacrificial framing of the Eucharist that `LS-111` already carries** (Heschmeyer's argument, RJ's own endorsing gloss). ⛔ **Do not deploy (a)-(d) without (e) in the same breath.**
+
+**(f) [Analysis] Justin's sense is non-propitiatory, and this is the datum most relevant to RJ specifically.** Justin's "sacrifice" is an **offering of praise and thanksgiving** — he nowhere describes a re-offering of Christ for sin, and the Eucharist-as-sacrifice claim he and the Didache both make is closer in KIND to **RJ's own stated position at `IP-39`** (Bramhall's eating-the-sacrificial-victim account affirmed; *"a fresh propitiatory sacrifice"* rejected on Hebrews, *"the book of Hebrews disagrees with you"*) **than to Rome's.** ⛔ **He cannot dismiss this material as "the Roman view" — on a non-propitiatory reading of "sacrifice," Justin's second-century sense sits nearer his own account than Rome's does.**
+
+**(g) TRANSLATIONAL CAUTION — AND IT CUTS AGAINST AN EARLIER ORCHESTRATION SUGGESTION; THE CORRECTION IS RECORDED, NOT JUST THE POINT.** Malachi 1:11's Hebrew word rendered "incense" is ***muqtar*** (a passive participle of *qatar*, "that which is burned / turned to smoke" — already logged above at §4.7 Jaw 3 and §4.8 Prong A under this name), **NOT *qetoret*** (the compound sanctuary incense of Exodus 30:34-38, of which *lebonah*/frankincense is one of four named components alongside stacte, onycha and galbanum). **⛔ *Lebonah* and *qetoret* are therefore NOT interchangeable, and this corpus must not treat them as such — confirmed by grep this pass, see the close-out.** ⚠️ **BUT THE FATHERS QUOTED ABOVE READ THE SEPTUAGINT, which renders *muqtar* as θυμίαμα (*thymiama*) — straightforwardly "incense."** So **the translational argument does NOT reach the patristic witness**: Justin and the Didache's authors were reading a Greek text that already says "incense," whatever the underlying Hebrew word technically denotes. **The translational point is therefore weaker than the typological argument in (a)-(d) above — it is available against a modern English-Bible-only argument from Malachi 1:11, but not against these patristic readings specifically.** Log both halves; do not run only the half that helps.
+
+**(h) ⏳ OPEN — NEEDS A REAL RESEARCH PASS, NOT ASSERTED HERE.** Whether the church of this period **burned incense in worship at all** is a separate question from how it read Malachi 1:11, and this note does not answer it. JD's cursory check this session found **nothing supporting pre-fourth-century Christian liturgical incense**, and **one unsupported Orthodox claim of apostolic origin** — neither checked further, neither asserted. ⚠️ **IF THAT HOLDS UNDER REAL RESEARCH:** these writers read Malachi 1:11 as already fulfilled in the Eucharistic assembly **while (on the available evidence) not themselves burning incense in worship** — which would make the fulfilment reading and the ceremonial-incense practice two separate, later-converging things rather than one continuous tradition. ⭐ **THE STRATEGIC CONSEQUENCE, RECORDED BECAUSE IT FEEDS ITEM 20/OQ20 ABOVE:** if RJ would not contest a fourth-century (or later) dating for Christian liturgical incense, the case against his incense practice **does not need to be historical at all** — his own `DQ-19`(a) burden rule requires only that a component be *received*, not that it be *apostolic*. A dating argument would therefore not even engage his stated rule. **NOT TO BE ASSERTED AS ESTABLISHED. This is a research lead, not a finding.**
+
+**Also checked this pass: Leviticus 2:1-2 against the corpus's Exodus 30 material.** Lev 2:1-2 places **frankincense (*lebonah*)** on the grain offering, burned with its memorial portion — a **separate, narrower use of frankincense specifically** from the compound sanctuary incense (*qetoret*) of Exodus 30:34-38, of which frankincense is one of four ingredients. ⚠️ **The premise cited to this pass — "an existing corpus note that frankincense is excluded from the sin offering (Lev 5:11) and the jealousy offering (Num 5:15)" — was checked and NOT FOUND anywhere in this corpus (`On_Incense_and_the_Altar.md`, this file, `Incense_Conversational_Outline.md`, grep-verified). Reported as a discrepancy rather than silently supplied: either the note exists somewhere this pass did not check, or the premise anticipated a note that has not yet been written. Not fabricated here.** ✅ **What WAS confirmed: nothing in the corpus's existing Malachi/Exodus 30/minchah material (§4.6, §4.7 Jaw 3, §4.8) anywhere treats *lebonah* (frankincense) and *qetoret* (the compound incense) as the same substance — the two are kept distinct wherever both appear**, and §4.8's Prong A already runs the *minchah*/grain-offering distinction this note would extend. A future pass may add the Lev 5:11/Num 5:15 exclusion material properly if JD confirms the note he had in mind, or supply it fresh as a §4.14.
