 **LS-112.** [Stated] Anchor: **§9 Eucharist — Sacrifice** ⭐⭐⭐ ; cross-ref `IP-39`, `IP-39a`, `IP-43`, `W44/Art-XXXI`, `QA-Art31` — ⭐⭐⭐ **BRAMHALL READ AT LENGTH, IN TWO SEPARATE QUOTATIONS FROM THE SAME PRIMARY SOURCE — THE FOUR-FOLD ANGLICAN ACCOUNT OF EUCHARISTIC SACRIFICE (COMMEMORATIVE / REPRESENTATIVE / IMPETRATIVE / APPLICATIVE), NEVER BEFORE IN THIS CORPUS AT THIS LENGTH OR WITH THIS TAXONOMY NAMED.** Reading from *Anglicanism*, ed. Moore & Cross, he quotes John Bramhall's 1653 reply to a French bishop — *"You say we have renounced your sacrifice of the Mass… we must give you leave to renounce your sacrifice indeed, and to adhere to the Apostle: by one offering he hath perfected forever them that are sanctified"* (s222-s228, t=1395.3-1418.8, [23:15]-[23:38]) — then a second Bramhall passage, read in full: *"we acknowledge an Eucharistical sacrifice of praise and thanksgiving, a commemorative sacrifice… a representative sacrifice… An impetrative sacrifice… and lastly, an applicative… sacrifice, or an application of his merits unto our souls"* (s236-s239, t=1490.5-1528.5, [24:50]-[25:28]), glossing the applicative sense as *"what is being focused upon in… Heschmeyer."* `SRC:` s222; s228; s236-s239. `DATE:` **2025-03-18.** **ATTRIBUTION: the Bramhall text is a 17th-century primary source READ ALOUD FROM A BOOK, quoted as Bramhall's, not as Rev. James's own composition — but the SELECTION, the framing (*"this is essentially the Anglican position"*), and the applicative-sense gloss connecting it to Heschmeyer's argument are his own.** ⭐ **Both renderings agree on every quoted clause.** **[Analysis]** ⭐⭐⭐ **§9's IP-39 already carries Bramhall's eating-the-victim account and the *"fresh propitiatory sacrifice… we disagree with you entirely"* line, so THIS ENTRY OVERLAPS `IP-39` ON THE BROAD BRAMHALL/EATING-THE-VICTIM POINT — flagged here in its own text, not only at the batch header. ⛔⛔ WHAT IS GENUINELY NEW: `IP-39` never gives the four-fold taxonomy (commemorativ